▪ Dings-Workflow

I describe the current Development Workflow for the All-Dings System. Historical
Day-specific Render Instructions are intentionally not part of this active
Contract.

▪▪ Development Entry

Start in the All-Dings Root and load the versioned
❲Dings-Bash-Rc❳❪14.md❫:

⌜⌜⌜bash
cd /path/to/All-Dings/111
source ./14.bashrc
⌟⌟⌟

Do this before running Tests, Builds or Dings Commands in a fresh interactive
or automated Shell. Do not reconstruct the Environment from remembered Chat
Context. The versioned Project Files are authoritative.

The Bootstrap provides at least:

◦ ‵Dings_Dir‵: All-Dings Root
◦ ‵Dings_Work_Directory‵: ‵$Dings_Dir/Work-Dir‵
◦ ‵PATH‵ access to Programs materialized in ‵Work-Dir‵
◦ ‵Dings_Bash_Executable‵: Preferred Bash, using ‵/opt/homebrew/bin/bash‵ when available
◦ Portable optional Bash-Completion loading

An explicitly supplied Environment Value remains authoritative.

‵Work-Dir‵ remains first so the materialized Dings Programs are authoritative.
On Apple Silicon, the interactive ‵bash‵ Alias and
‵Dings_Bash_Executable‵ prefer ‵/opt/homebrew/bin/bash‵ over macOS
‵/bin/bash‵. Automated Commands use ‵$Dings_Bash_Executable‵ explicitly.
Other Platforms fall back to the Bash already resolved through ‵PATH‵.

The complete ‵/opt/homebrew/bin‵ Directory remains available at the end of
‵PATH‵. It is not placed before the verified Project Python because doing so
would silently replace macOS Python 3.9 and its installed Runtime Dependencies
with a different Homebrew Python.

▪▪ Collaboration References

In Chat, use the Dings-canonical Markdown Shape with an extensionless Dings
Number:

⌜⌜⌜markdown
[Dings-Feature-Ensure-Consistency](300101012)
⌟⌟⌟

This keeps the Reference clickable in the rendered Conversation. When Chat UI
Presentation hides the Target Number, copying the Conversation into a plain
Text Editor such as Vim recovers the complete Markdown Reference. Vim may be
used as a lossless Intermediate for inspecting, editing and copying the Source
Text. If a Composer converts the pasted Reference into a structured Link
Element, edit it in Vim before pasting the finished Text.

Use a Code Block only when the Reference Syntax itself is the Subject. In
canonical Dings Markdown Sources, use the real ‵.md‵ Target. In DMD Sources,
use the corresponding DMD Reference Syntax. Do not introduce a parallel
‵Label#Number‵ Notation merely for Chat.

▪▪ Version Blogs And Work-Items

A Version Blog is the ordered Planning and Integration Context for one
Development Version. Its direct ❲Member❳❪300044026.md❫ Statements select the
Work-Items assigned to that Version. Editorial Membership in the general
❲All-Dings-Development-Blog❳❪300100006.md❫ provides discoverability but is not
a Version Assignment.

Work has three independent Axes:

⌜⌜⌜text
Work-Item Identity
    Stable Dings Identity of the Work

Version Membership
    Explicit direct Membership in a Version Blog

State
    Global Lifecycle of the Work-Item
⌟⌟⌟

A small Work-Item is a ❲Dings_Blog_Entry❳❪300040013.md❫ with the additional
❲Todo❳❪60203.md❫ and ❲Work-Item❳❪120000001.md❫ Roles. It may additionally be
a ❲Feature❳❪60204.md❫. A structured Work-Item is instead a recursive
❲Dings_Blog❳❪300040012.md❫ with the same Work Roles and may contain
Requirements, Inventory, Dog-Food, Achievements and Follow-Ups Entries. Blog
and Blog-Entry remain mutually exclusive main Roles.

The V1 Lifecycle uses:

◦ ❲State_New❳❪60206.md❫: Prepared but not begun
◦ ❲State_Started❳❪60207.md❫: Begun and not done
◦ ❲State_Done❳❪60205.md❫: Completed

‵Todo‵ is a durable Work Role and is not removed when its State becomes Done.
A Version-Neutral ‵State_New‵ Work-Item may exist without Membership in a
Version Blog. Selecting it for one Version adds the same Work-Item Identity as
an ordered direct Member; it does not copy or rename the Work-Item.

The current Version Blog has at most one direct Work-Item in
‵State_Started‵. Current Focus is derived from direct Version Membership,
Work-Item Role and Started State. No separate Focus Store is maintained.

If unstarted Work is removed from a Version Plan, its State remains New. If
started Work crosses a Version Boundary, its original Membership remains
historically relevant and the next Version may reference the same Identity.
Because State is global, the State at Version Close is initially preserved as
normal Version-Blog Decision Content. A Version-Bound Snapshot Ontology is
introduced only when real Query Requirements justify it.

▪▪ Work Command Boundary

‵dings-gpt work‵ is a transparent Compiler from Workflow
Convenience Syntax to general Dings Operations. It has no private Store,
Index, State, Query Language, Manifest Mutation, Number Allocation or File
Creation Logic.

General Query, Create, Bundle, Manifest and Projection Capabilities remain
available outside the Workflow Wrapper. Shared Library Functions are correct;
the Wrapper need not launch Shell Subprocesses. Workflow-specific
Orchestration and Validation may be implemented by the Wrapper, but the
underlying Data Operations must not be exclusive to it.

The effective Plan follows:

⌜⌜⌜text
Resolve
    -> Query
    -> Validate
    -> Mutate
    -> Project
⌟⌟⌟

‵-explain‵ exposes this generic Plan. The implemented V30 Surface is:

⌜⌜⌜text
work list [BLOG]             Query open Work globally or in one Blog Scope
work graph                   Project the Work-Item State Machine
work new NAME                Create a public system Work-Item in State_New
work new -kind=todo NAME     Create a private personal Todo in State_New
work start WORK_ITEM         State_New     -> State_Started
work done WORK_ITEM          State_Started -> State_Done
work reopen WORK_ITEM        State_Done    -> State_Started
⌟⌟⌟

❲Dings_App_State_Machine❳❪300042033.md❫ validates each requested Transition
against a ❲Dings_SM_Graph❳❪300044087.md❫. A separate general Manifest
Primitive atomically replaces the one expected authored State Object. A Start
or Reopen additionally rejects a second direct Started Work-Item in the same
Version Blog.

‵work new‵ compiles two Workflow Profiles into the general
‵dings-gpt dings new‵ Operation. A public ‵work-item‵ is owned by Repository
‵300000007‵ and allocates monotonically in the authored ‵Work-Items‵ Range
‵300101013-300101999‵. A private personal ‵todo‵ is owned by Repository
‵0-secrets-local‵, allocates in the authored ‵TODOS‵ Range
‵1971200000-1971209000‵ and creates a sibling Access Source containing User
‵0‵. Both Profiles author exactly one ‵State_New‵ Statement and a Create Time.
Public Work defaults Scope to ❲Dings-System❳❪300000007.md❫; personal Todos do
not invent a Local Scope Statement.

The general Create Primitive reads the authored Ranges from ‵0.txt‵, checks
the candidate against all Index Entries and numbered Repository Sources, uses
a Root-local creation Lock, refuses overwrites, atomically replaces the
expected Index Text and creates each Source without replacement. A handled
multi-File Failure rolls back unchanged newly created Sources and the exact
Index Mutation. Historical duplicate Entries in ‵0.txt‵ are not silently
rewritten; they do not permit allocation of the same Number again.

Creation does not add Blog Membership, update Work-Dir, build derived Views or
create a Git Commit. Those remain explicit later Operations. In particular,
ordered Blog Membership belongs to the Blog Domain rather than to
‵work new‵.

The global Backlog is the Query projection of Work-Items in ‵State_New‵, not
an authored Blog and not an implicit Membership in the Development Blog.
‵work list -state=State_New‵ therefore finds both assigned and unassigned Work
by stable identity. A Version-Blog Membership means explicit Version planning;
another Blog Membership means an independently authored context. Neither is
inferred merely because an Item is new.

‵work list‵ accepts the optional Blog Scope as a positional Dings. Resolution
validates the general authored ‵Blog‵ Role, so no separate Type Flag is required:

⌜⌜⌜bash
dings-gpt work list 300100149
⌟⌟⌟

The older ‵-blog BLOG‵ form remains an equivalent explicit spelling. If both
forms are supplied, they must resolve to the same stable Dings Identity.
Transition Commands retain ‵-blog‵ because their positional Dings is already
the Work-Item.

▪▪ Blog Command Boundary

‵dings-gpt blog‵ is the Blog-Domain Wrapper over the same general Dings
Primitives. Its implemented Surface is:

⌜⌜⌜text
blog list [BLOG]              List Blogs and Entries or one recursive Member Tree
blog new NAME                 Create one Bundle-capable Blog in the authored Blogs Range
blog add BLOG MEMBER          Append one direct ordered Membership
blog add BLOG MEMBER -before EXISTING
blog add BLOG MEMBER -after EXISTING
blog remove BLOG MEMBER       Remove exactly one direct Membership
⌟⌟⌟

‵blog list‵ maps to the ordinary ‵show sm list Blog‵ and
‵dings-gpt dings members‵ Queries. ‵blog new‵ compiles a Blog Profile into
the general authored Dings Creation Operation and allocates in
‵Blogs (300102000-300102999)‵. The Profile authors the general ‵Blog‵ Role
plus the separate ‵Dings_Bundle‵ capability. It does not add the new Blog to
another Blog.

The global Tree uses ‵Blog‵ as its conceptual Root and preserves direct
Member order below every Blog. It includes leaf Entries and recursively
expands Member Dings that also resolve as Blogs. The historical
‵Dings_Blog‵ Role remains a compatible specialization, not the public filter
of the Command.

Add and Remove target the direct Member Sequence of the explicitly addressed
Blog identity. Add appends by default; ‵-before‵ and ‵-after‵ name one existing
direct Member as the placement Anchor. Remove requires exactly one direct
Member identity. Neither Command guesses a recursive occurrence. A future
Mutation of one contextual recursive Occurrence requires an explicit
Bundle-Occurrence Address rather than a Dings-identity heuristic.

The shared ordered Bundle Mutation preserves unrelated Manifest Text, rejects
duplicate direct Membership and invalid Anchors, compares the exact expected
Source and performs a same-directory atomic replacement under a Root-local
Bundle Lock. Blog adds additionally validate Blog and Member Roles, reject
Blog-to-Blog cycles and validate the hypothetical direct Started Work-Item
count when the target is a Version Blog.

Blog commands do not change Work State, build or commit. ‵-explain‵ names the
general Query, Creation or Membership Operations and prints equivalent public
‵show sm‵ and ‵dings members‵ Queries where available.

▪▪ Git Branch Context

The active Git Branch is a Resolver Input and Safety Context for Workflow
Commands. For example, Branch ‵v30‵ may resolve exactly one Version Blog named
‵V30‵ and a Mutation may verify that its explicit Target belongs to that
Version Context.

The Branch is not persistent Workflow Truth. An explicit Version-Blog
Argument is authoritative. Missing, conflicting or ambiguous Branch-to-Blog
Resolution is an Error. Work-Item Identity, Membership and State remain in
ordinary Dings Manifests.

▪▪ Conversation Context And Handoff

Chat Content is a Source, Decision Provenance and Handoff Input. It is not an
authoritative Workflow Store. Durable Decisions are distilled into Work-Item
Content and normal Manifest Statements while the Source Context remains
identifiable.

A future Workflow Convenience Command may resolve the active Work-Item,
consume an addressed Chat Channel and compose a Handoff through general
Channel, Create, Bundle and Manifest Operations. It must not create a private
Chat Database or treat every conversational Statement as authoritative.

▪▪ Source And Materialization Boundary

Edit canonical Files in their owning Git Repository below
‵.Dings/Repositories/‵ or in the special Root Repository. Do not edit derived
‵Work-Dir‵, ‵Day‵, ‵*.ir.json‵, ‵*.sm1.json‵ or ‵*.sm2.json‵ Files as Sources.

After adding, removing or renaming Source Dings, update the materialized
Work-Dir:

⌜⌜⌜bash
dings-gpt workdir update
⌟⌟⌟

▪▪ Tests

The complete Dings-System Test Suite is executed directly by File Path:

⌜⌜⌜bash
python3 .Dings/Repositories/300000007/300100005.test.py
⌟⌟⌟

The numeric Dings File Name is not a Python Module Identifier. Therefore do not
use ‵python3 -m unittest 300100005.test...‵. A focused Test is addressed through
the direct Script Runner:

⌜⌜⌜bash
python3 .Dings/Repositories/300000007/300100005.test.py \
  Dings_Lld_Format_Tests.test_v29_datetimeoriginal_becomes_one_zone_less_source_near_claim
⌟⌟⌟

The Runner imports ‵Dings_Gpt_Runtime.py‵ and uses the repository-local Python,
Matplotlib and XDG Cache Contract. Standalone Python Tools that do not import
the Dings Runtime must set an explicit cache Location or perform a cache-free
Check as described by ❲All-Dings-LLD❳❪300100005.md❫.

▪▪ Build Verification

Run the complete incremental Document Build:

⌜⌜⌜bash
dings-gpt build render
⌟⌟⌟

Then run it once more. A stable checkout must report the no-op Result:

⌜⌜⌜text
Build State: Current Nodes=... Edges=...
⌟⌟⌟

The canonical Pipeline remains:

⌜⌜⌜text
md/dmd -> ir -> sm1 -> sm2 -> html
⌟⌟⌟

▪▪ Review And Commit

Before committing:

◦ Inspect the owning Repository Status and Diff
◦ Preserve unrelated User Changes
◦ Run proportionate focused Tests and the complete Suite for shared Runtime Changes
◦ Verify the full Build and its repeated Current Result
◦ Do not record generated Runtime Paths or machine-local Cache Paths in SM

Commit each changed Repository separately on the active Development Branch.
The Commit Message describes the semantic Result. Generated Work-Dir and Day
Artifacts are not committed as canonical Sources.

▪▪ Installation And Host Setup

Machine installation is maintained separately by
❲Dings-Mac-Book-Install-Howto❳❪300030007.md❫. Docker and Server Deployment are
not prerequisites for ordinary local Dings-System Tests and Document Builds.
